# 06-AddOverlapTargets

## Overview

Create an invisible interaction target where two RGB spheres overlap.

The overlap target allows the user to interact with the mixed color
without directly manipulating the two original spheres.

### Detect Sphere Overlap

Use the distance between movable spheres to determine whether they
overlap.

When two spheres are close enough, calculate the midpoint between them.
This midpoint becomes the position of the overlap target.

@Code(name: "SphereOverlapSystem.swift", file: "SphereOverlapSystem.swift")

The `SphereOverlapSystem` checks every pair of movable spheres and
compares the distance between their world positions with the sphere
diameter.

When two spheres overlap, the system calculates their mixed RGB color,
midpoint, and a unique key for the pair.

### Create the Overlap Target

Create an invisible `ModelEntity` at the midpoint of the overlapping
spheres.

The entity does not need a visible mesh. Instead, it uses a
`CollisionComponent` and `InputTargetComponent` so it can receive
manipulation input.

@Code(
name: "Create the overlap target",
file: "SphereInteractionManager.swift"
)

The overlap target also receives a `ManipulationComponent` so it can
follow the user's manipulation gesture.

### Store Overlap Information

Use `OverlapVisualComponent` to store information about the overlap.

The component keeps the overlap key and the mixed RGB color associated
with that target.

@Code(
name: "OverlapVisualComponent.swift",
file: "OverlapVisualComponent.swift"
)

The overlap key identifies which pair of spheres produced the target.
The stored color is used when the user interacts with the target to
create the mixed sphere.

### Create the Mixed Sphere

When the user begins manipulating an overlap target, create a new
sphere using the calculated mixed color.

@Code(
name: "Create the mixed sphere",
file: "SphereInteractionManager.swift"
)

The mixed sphere is positioned at the overlap point and added to the
collection of movable spheres.

Because it is treated as a movable sphere, the resulting color can
participate in future interactions with other spheres.

### Keep the Target Updated

The overlap targets are updated whenever a sphere is moved or
released.

Targets that no longer correspond to an overlap are removed, while
new overlapping pairs create new targets.

This keeps the invisible interaction points synchronized with the
current positions of the movable spheres.
